## 7. Comparing TB1 and TB2

Both testbenches use the same performance measurement equations and boundaries.

The main difference is the forwarding architecture.

### TB1 — Stage-1 forwarding

- Uses `systolic_array`.
- Instantiates `pe_overflow`.
- A row \(r\) starts at cycle \(2r\).
- A column \(c\) starts at cycle \(2c\).
- The testbench schedule accounts for a two-cycle PE-to-PE forwarding hop.

### TB2 — Stage-0 forwarding

- Uses `systolic_array_forwarding`.
- Instantiates `pe_overflow_forwarding`.
- A row \(r\) starts at cycle \(r\).
- A column \(c\) starts at cycle \(c\).
- The testbench schedule accounts for a one-cycle PE-to-PE forwarding hop.

### Comparison methodology

| Comparison parameter | TB1 | TB2 |
|---|---|---|
| Forwarding stage | Stage 1 | Stage 0 |
| PE-to-PE hop | 2 cycles | 1 cycle |
| Matrix dimensions | 2×2 | 2×2 |
| Reduction dimension | 2 | 2 |
| Input data | Identical | Identical |
| Clock period | 10 ns | 10 ns |
| Performance start | First valid external input | First valid external input |
| Performance end | Last valid PE output | Last valid PE output |
| Throughput calculation | Total MACs / latency | Total MACs / latency |

The comparison allows us to evaluate how the forwarding architecture influences total GEMM execution latency and average throughput.

